WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.797
Picture cooking your best dish for a friend's party.

2
00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:08.239
At home, it comes out perfect every time.

3
00:00:08.489 --> 00:00:14.533
At your friend's house, the oven runs hotter, there is no fresh garlic, and the salt is a different kind.

4
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:19.035
The dish fails, and you stand in their kitchen saying: but it works at home.

5
00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:21.637
Software has the same problem.

6
00:00:21.637 --> 00:00:29.321
Your test passes on your Mac with Python 3.14, and fails on the CI machine, which has Python 3.12.

7
00:00:29.321 --> 00:00:31.203
Nobody touched the code.

8
00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:33.477
Everything around it changed.

9
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:38.107
Wouldn't it be nice to carry the kitchen along with the dish?

10
00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:42.156
Pack it like a lunchbox: the meal plus its own fork, salt and napkin.

11
00:00:42.406 --> 00:00:48.663
Into the box goes the app, the runtime that runs its code, the libraries it uses, and its settings.

12
00:00:48.913 --> 00:00:50.062
Then you seal it.

13
00:00:50.062 --> 00:00:53.441
That sealed, read-only package is called an image.

14
00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:56.955
Docker is the tool that builds images and runs them.

15
00:00:57.205 --> 00:01:02.957
And the same image runs the same way on your Mac, on a CI machine, and on a cloud server.

16
00:01:02.957 --> 00:01:05.154
The kitchen travels with the dish.

17
00:01:07.004 --> 00:01:08.950
Now open one box and run it.

18
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.895
So what is actually running?

19
00:01:10.895 --> 00:01:13.467
A tiny computer inside your computer?

20
00:01:13.717 --> 00:01:20.768
That is a fair guess, because there is an older way that works exactly like that: a virtual machine, or VM.

21
00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:23.074
It pretends to be a whole computer.

22
00:01:23.324 --> 00:01:26.679
It boots a full operating system with its own kernel.

23
00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:33.705
The kernel is the core of an operating system: it starts programs, hands out memory, and talks to the hardware.

24
00:01:33.955 --> 00:01:36.225
Booting all of that takes many seconds.

25
00:01:36.225 --> 00:01:39.601
If a container were that, it would be a busy little place.

26
00:01:39.851 --> 00:01:40.896
Let's test it.

27
00:01:40.896 --> 00:01:43.061
Alpine is a tiny Linux image.

28
00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:46.271
We ask it to list every process it can see.

29
00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:49.364
A real computer runs hundreds of programs.

30
00:01:49.364 --> 00:01:51.666
How many will this container list?

31
00:01:51.666 --> 00:01:52.749
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:54.999 --> 00:01:55.323
One.

33
00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:58.003
Just ps itself, running as PID 1.

34
00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:04.661
A PID is the number Linux gives each running program, and 1 goes to the first one.

35
00:02:04.911 --> 00:02:07.214
Well, that is not a busy computer.

36
00:02:07.214 --> 00:02:09.042
That is one lonely program.

37
00:02:09.792 --> 00:02:11.602
Here is what really happens.

38
00:02:11.602 --> 00:02:14.704
Down at the bottom is the kernel of the machine.

39
00:02:14.704 --> 00:02:18.388
Above it run lots of ordinary programs, called processes.

40
00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.022
A container is one more of those.

41
00:02:21.022 --> 00:02:22.684
A normal Linux process.

42
00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.357
No second computer, no second kernel.

43
00:02:25.607 --> 00:02:28.366
But the kernel gives it two extra things.

44
00:02:28.366 --> 00:02:29.241
First, walls.

45
00:02:29.241 --> 00:02:34.691
Like a lunchbox in the office fridge: the food inside cannot see the other boxes.

46
00:02:34.691 --> 00:02:37.047
Linux calls these walls namespaces.

47
00:02:37.297 --> 00:02:44.630
Inside its walls, the process gets its own list of programs, its own network, its own name, and its own files.

48
00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.697
That is why ps saw only itself.

49
00:02:46.947 --> 00:02:48.123
Second, a budget.

50
00:02:48.123 --> 00:02:51.929
A cap on how much processor time and memory it may use.

51
00:02:51.929 --> 00:02:55.457
Linux calls this a cgroup, short for control group.

52
00:02:55.707 --> 00:02:58.807
Go past the memory cap, and the kernel kills it.

53
00:02:59.657 --> 00:03:03.315
So a container is a process, with walls and a budget.

54
00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:04.695
Not a tiny computer.

55
00:03:04.695 --> 00:03:09.043
A process that was told, very firmly, to mind its own business.

56
00:03:09.693 --> 00:03:12.700
Start two more containers, and here is the twist.

57
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.855
They all use the same kernel, like lunches that all go through one office microwave.

58
00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:19.461
Nothing has to boot.

59
00:03:19.461 --> 00:03:22.172
So a container starts in about a second.

60
00:03:22.822 --> 00:03:24.660
Now a puzzle for Mac users.

61
00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.791
A Mac has its own kernel, and it is not Linux.

62
00:03:27.791 --> 00:03:30.378
So which kernel runs these containers?

63
00:03:30.378 --> 00:03:31.467
Pause and guess.

64
00:03:33.622 --> 00:03:35.804
The Linux kernel of one small VM.

65
00:03:35.804 --> 00:03:44.067
Docker Desktop, the Mac app that installs Docker, quietly starts it in the background, and every container shares its kernel.
